    <h1>COFFEE “ON TAP” IS THE FUTURE</h1>
    <p class="muted">By John Goerke. Original article, with a later update.</p>

    <p>Coffee on tap is moving from a niche idea to the mainstream. Large coffee chains have adopted nitro cold brew served on tap, and tap coffee is now a familiar sight for customers.</p>

    <h2>Why on tap coffee works for restaurants and bars</h2>
    <p>Serving nitro coffee on tap is operationally simple. A restaurant, bar or cafe pours cold brew on tap straight from a keg, with no brewing, batching or ice to manage during service. The same tap can also serve nitro tea on tap.</p>

    <h2>A keg that is preserved and shelf stable</h2>
    <p>Bona Fide nitro coffee kegs are preserved and shelf stable, so a keg does not have to sit in a cooler while it waits to be tapped. That makes cold brew on tap practical for venues without much refrigerated space.</p>

    <h2>How Bona Fide brews and kegs coffee for the tap</h2>
    <p>Bona Fide brews the coffee cold and packs it into kegs ready to tap. Learn more about <a href="/nitro-coffee-kegs/">nitro coffee kegs</a>, the <a href="/kegerators/">equipment to serve nitro on tap</a> and <a href="/nitro-cold-brew/">nitro cold brew</a>.</p>

    <p>Ready to put coffee on tap? <a href="/contact-bona-fide-nitro-coffee-and-tea/">Contact us</a> or see <a href="/howtobuy/">How to Buy</a>.</p>
